  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-IP24-MWI152</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhanced and extended coverage of social protection to vulnerable groups</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Malawi&#x2019;s Social Protection System offers limited protection both in terms of provisions and coverage of the population. The legal framework is limited to contributory social protection with insurance-based provisions only for Old Age, while work injury, paternal benefits and sick leave are based on employer liability schemes. The country programme outcome is contributing towards increase the coverage and quality of social protection system in Malawi by strengthening the social protection policy and legal frameworks to facilitate a rights-based approach to social protection. The ILO is promoting decent job creation and extension of social protection through implementation of the Global Accelerator on Jobs and Social Protection National Roadmap aimed at accelerating employment creation and extension of social protection in the agriculture, mining and tourism sectors. The ILO is supporting development of a sustainable financing for social protection supporting development of a sustainable financing strategy for social protection to facilitate long-term sustainability of the social protection system in Malawi. The intervention is also building the capacity of government, social partners and national partners to design and deliver comprehensive social protection programmes through capacity building interventions on International social security standards and the TRANSFORM Package on Building and Managing Social Protection Floors. 

The beneficiaries include Government Ministries, Social Partners, Development Partners, beneficiaries of social assistance programmes, population in active labour market, and Civil Society Organisations. 

The Partners involved are Ministry of Labour, Ministry of Finance and Economic Affairs, Ministry of Gender and Social Welfare, Employers Organisation, Workers Organisation, Civil Society Social Protection Network, UN Women, Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO), and United Nations International Children&#x2019;s Emergency Fund (UNICEF).</narrative>
  </description>
